# Conservation Renewal Agenda

## v0.6.2 — Ecological Restoration Research Framework

### 1. Purpose

Ecological restoration is one of the most visible forms of conservation.

It includes efforts to:

* restore degraded rivers;
* reconnect fragmented habitat;
* recover forests;
* restore wetlands;
* rebuild grasslands;
* remove invasive species;
* rehabilitate mined lands;
* restore coastal ecosystems;
* recover fish and wildlife habitat;
* improve post-fire landscapes.

Yet restoration presents a recurring measurement problem.

A project can be:

* funded;
* designed;
* permitted;
* constructed;
* completed;
* monitored;

without necessarily producing durable ecological recovery.

The central question for this pillar is therefore:

> **Can American restoration institutions consistently convert restoration investments into measurable, ecologically meaningful, and durable improvements in ecosystem condition?**

