# 22. Restoration at Landscape Scale

Many restoration projects are individually small relative to the ecological system.

A landscape may require:

* hundreds of projects;
* multiple landowners;
* multiple agencies;
* repeated treatments;
* long-term maintenance.

This creates a distinction between:

### Project restoration

Individual site improvement.

### Program restoration

Multiple coordinated projects.

### Landscape restoration

Projects designed around an ecological system.

The project should investigate whether moving from project-based restoration to landscape-scale planning improves ecological outcomes.

---

# 23. Restoration Bottlenecks

The existing bottleneck framework applies:

### Knowledge

Insufficient understanding of ecosystem processes.

### Diagnosis

Incorrect identification of the limiting factor.

### Authority

No institution controls the relevant pressure.

### Capacity

Insufficient technical or financial resources.

### Coordination

Projects are not connected.

### Ecological

Natural constraints limit recovery.

### Allocation

Competing land or water uses restrict restoration.

### Durability

Gains are not maintained.

Restoration therefore provides another setting in which the bottleneck framework can be tested.

